@app.get('/dynamics-force-training', response_class=HTMLResponse)
def dynamics_force_training_v47() -> HTMLResponse:
    body = _v47_nav() + """
    <section id="top" class="panel">
      <h2>Dynamics and Force Core Training Baseline — v47</h2>
      <p>This page lists the force/dynamics skills that should be trained and tested after the v46 kinematics behavior inside v47.</p>
      <h3>Training sequence</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Meaning of force:</strong> force as an interaction; contact forces and field forces.</li>
        <li><strong>Free-body diagrams:</strong> choose one object; include only forces acting on that object.</li>
        <li><strong>Force in one dimension:</strong> choose +x; write signed equations such as pull − friction = ma.</li>
        <li><strong>Weight and normal force:</strong> distinguish mg from N; avoid assuming F_n = mg in all cases.</li>
        <li><strong>Friction:</strong> kinetic friction f_k = μ_kN; static friction f_s ≤ μ_sN.</li>
        <li><strong>Tension:</strong> force along a string or rope; same ideal tension only under ideal rope/pulley assumptions.</li>
        <li><strong>Force in two dimensions:</strong> resolve angled forces; write ΣF_x = ma_x and ΣF_y = ma_y.</li>
        <li><strong>Inclined planes:</strong> choose axes parallel/perpendicular to surface; use mg sinθ and mg cosθ components.</li>
        <li><strong>Force in three dimensions:</strong> use vector components F_x, F_y, F_z and write three component equations.</li>
        <li><strong>Applying Newton's laws:</strong> connect motion and force; identify first, second, and third law situations.</li>
        <li><strong>Equilibrium:</strong> translational equilibrium uses ΣF = 0; static equilibrium later adds Στ = 0.</li>
        <li><strong>Misconception diagnosis:</strong> force is not required to maintain constant velocity; third-law pairs act on different objects; normal force is not always mg.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>First v47 test prompts</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Train me on force in one dimension.</li>
        <li>A 4 kg cart has a net force of 12 N. What is its acceleration?</li>
        <li>What is the weight of a 5 kg object near Earth?</li>
        <li>Explain normal force. Is N always equal to mg?</li>
        <li>Train me on force in two dimensions on an incline.</li>
        <li>Explain equilibrium. Does equilibrium mean no forces act?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="actions"><a class="button" href="/physics-ai-chat-v47?fresh=1">Open Branch 1 for Testing</a><a class="button secondary" href="/student-ai-chat-v47">Branch Selector</a></p>
    </section>
    """
    return page('Dynamics Force Training v47', body)


